**Interpretation.** Evenly distributing `limit` calls over 60 seconds means one call every `interval = 60000 / limit` ms. So instead of rejecting extra calls, the limiter **queues** them and releases one per interval. Confirm with the interviewer whether excess calls should be queued, dropped or rejected.

**Closure-based solution (queue with fixed spacing):**

```js
function rateLimiter(func, limit) {
  const interval = 60000 / limit;
  const queue = [];
  let timer = null;

  function drain() {
    const job = queue.shift();
    job.resolve(func(...job.args));       // or wrap in try/catch and reject
    if (queue.length === 0) { timer = null; return; }
    timer = setTimeout(drain, interval);
  }

  return function (...args) {
    return new Promise((resolve) => {
      queue.push({ args, resolve });
      if (!timer) {                         // idle: run the first call now
        timer = setTimeout(drain, 0);
      }
    });
  };
}
```

`queue`, `timer` and `interval` live in the closure, so each limiter instance has private state. Each call returns a promise that settles when its turn comes. Time per call is O(1).

**A fix for realism.** Wrap `func` in try/catch (or `Promise.resolve().then(() => func(...))`) so one failed call rejects only its own promise. Track the last execution time so a call arriving after a long idle period runs immediately without waiting.

**Alternative: sliding-window limiter.** Keep timestamps of the last `limit` calls; allow a call if fewer than `limit` timestamps fall inside the last 60 seconds, otherwise reject or delay. This allows bursts up to `limit`, so it does not satisfy "evenly distributed" but is the common textbook rate limiter. A **token bucket** is similar: refill at `limit/60` tokens per second with a bucket capacity.

**Edge cases:** validate `limit > 0`, cap queue length for backpressure, preserve call order (FIFO), and `this` binding if `func` is a method.
